From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Charles Pellegrini <c4ffein.work@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:54:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH v3 5/6] lib: scatterlist: sg_split(): reject nb_splits <= 0
 and a negative skip

sg_split() does not check its scalar arguments:

 - nb_splits == 0: the splitters array is a zero-size allocation
   (ZERO_SIZE_PTR), and sg_calculate_split() reads split_sizes[0] and
   reads and writes splitters[0] before it looks at nb_splits: an
   out-of-bounds access (a heap-buffer-overflow in a userspace ASan
   build).

 - nb_splits < 0: the count passed to kzalloc_objs() is negative, so the
   allocation size overflows; the call fails and sg_split() reports
   -ENOMEM for what is an invalid argument.

 - skip < 0: skip is an off_t (a long) compared with an unsigned int
   entry length. On 64-bit the comparison is signed, so a negative skip
   never skips anything and becomes a negative offset into the first
   entry. With a 10-byte entry at page offset 100, skip = -3 and
   sizes = {5}, out[0] starts at offset 97, three bytes before the input,
   and sg_split() returns 0. On 32-bit, long and unsigned int compare as
   unsigned, so a negative skip already looks like a skip past the end of
   @in and sg_split() returns -EINVAL; the new check gives 64-bit the same
   result.

The kernel-doc does not rule out any of them. Return -EINVAL for all three
before allocating anything, and say so in the kernel-doc.

Fixes: f8bcbe62acd0 ("lib: scatterlist: add sg splitting function")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5 hegel-c
---
 lib/sg_split.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/sg_split.c b/lib/sg_split.c
index 1904dd609..70a9eb557 100644
--- a/lib/sg_split.c
+++ b/lib/sg_split.c
@@ -186,8 +186,9 @@ static void sg_split_mapped(struct sg_splitter *splitters, const int nb_splits)
  * mapped), and its @out_mapped_nents is 1.
  * It will be the caller's duty to kfree() out array members.
  *
- * Returns 0 upon success, -EINVAL if the request runs past the end of @in,
- * or -ENOMEM.  On failure, @out and @out_mapped_nents are left untouched.
+ * Returns 0 upon success, -EINVAL if @nb_splits is not positive, @skip is
+ * negative or the request runs past the end of @in, or -ENOMEM.  On
+ * failure, @out and @out_mapped_nents are left untouched.
  */
 int sg_split(struct scatterlist *in, const int in_mapped_nents,
 	     const off_t skip, const int nb_splits,
@@ -198,6 +199,9 @@ int sg_split(struct scatterlist *in, const int in_mapped_nents,
 	int i, ret;
 	struct sg_splitter *splitters;
 
+	if (nb_splits <= 0 || skip < 0)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
 	splitters = kzalloc_objs(*splitters, nb_splits, gfp_mask);
 	if (!splitters)
 		return -ENOMEM;
